---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';

const keywords =
	'Fhugawz, thirteen Worlds, song sequencing, album context, song placement, creative process, music production, narrative music project, artist journal, world building';
const excerpt =
	'Notes on what happens when a song works on its own but still has not found the right place inside the larger sequence.';
const heroImage = '/images/blog/blog-from-demo-to-direction-music-production-fhugawz.webp.webp';
const imageBase = '/images/blog/songs-need-more-than-production';
---

<ArticleLayout
	title="When a Song Finds Its Place — Fhugawz"
	description={excerpt}
	spanishTitle="Cuando una canción encuentra su lugar — Fhugawz"
	spanishDescription="Notas sobre lo que ocurre cuando una canción funciona por sí sola, pero todavía no ha encontrado el lugar correcto dentro de la secuencia más amplia."
	keywords={keywords}
	type="article"
	image={heroImage}
	imageAlt="A solitary figure considering where a song belongs inside the larger Fhugawz sequence."
	heroImage={heroImage}
	heroKicker="PROJECT DIARY"
	heroLede={excerpt}
>
	<div class="article-song-place">
		<p>Today I was organizing some songs between the Worlds and something happened again that has been happening quite a lot lately: I listened to a song I like, but in the place where I had put it, it simply did not work.</p>
		<p>The song was not bad.</p>
		<p>That was exactly the problem.</p>
		<p>For a long time I think I would have tried to solve something like that inside the song itself. Change the production, move a section, find another sound, add something that would finally make it fit.</p>
		<p>Now I am starting to consider another possibility.</p>
		<p>Maybe the song is fine and it is simply in the wrong place.</p>

		<h2>Finishing a song does not always mean understanding it</h2>
		<p>There are songs I can listen to for months without being completely sure where they belong inside the project.</p>
		<p>I know what I like about them. I have a general idea of how I want them to sound and I can imagine some of their images, but something still does not completely settle.</p>
		<p>Then something strange happens.</p>
		<p>I move the song to another World, listen to it next to different tracks, and suddenly I understand things that I could not see before.</p>
		<p>A lyric that felt too abstract begins to make sense. A sound that seemed out of place stops bothering me. Sometimes I even understand better why the song needed to feel a certain way.</p>
		<p>The song did not change.</p>
		<p>The context did.</p>
		<p>That has made me think quite a lot about how much a song depends on what surrounds it.</p>

		<div class="article-image">
			<img src={`${imageBase}/demo-song-creative-direction-independent-artist.webp`} alt="A song searching for its place within the larger Fhugawz sequence." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
		</div>

		<h2>Order starts changing meaning</h2>
		<p>Now that the records form a sequence, placing one song before or after another is no longer only a musical decision.</p>
		<p>It can change the way I understand what the song is saying.</p>
		<p>A song about isolation does not mean exactly the same thing at the beginning of a story as it does after the character has already lost something important. Something that could feel hopeful on its own can become unsettling when I know what comes next.</p>
		<p>I like that possibility a lot.</p>
		<p>There is information I do not need to put directly into the lyrics because it can exist between songs.</p>
		<p>Even the silence between two tracks can end up doing part of the work.</p>
		<p>I am still learning to think this way because for a long time my relationship with songs was much more individual. I would finish one, begin another, and each one had to carry almost all of its meaning by itself.</p>
		<p>Now they can speak to each other.</p>

		<h2>Sometimes the production belongs to the place too</h2>
		<p>This is also starting to affect the way I produce the songs.</p>
		<p>Not because I think there is one correct way to do it. A lot of this process is still me trying things because I am learning what works for me.</p>
		<p>But I have noticed that I am beginning to think about different kinds of problems.</p>
		<p>There are productions I really like when I hear them on their own, but inside a World they suddenly feel too large. Or too clean. A drum sound can work perfectly well and still make the song feel as if it came from another record.</p>
		<p>That has happened to me several times while organizing the material.</p>
		<p>Before, I probably would have assumed I needed a better mix.</p>
		<p>Now sometimes I realize I need a different decision.</p>
		<p>Maybe the voice needs to feel closer. Maybe I do not need as many layers. A slightly imperfect guitar might communicate the character’s state better than another recording that is technically much cleaner.</p>
		<p>I do not have a rule for that.</p>
		<p>I am figuring it out song by song.</p>

		<div class="article-image">
			<img src={`${imageBase}/music-production-arrangement-song-identity.webp`} alt="Production choices being reconsidered in relation to the World around a song." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
		</div>

		<h2>Each World starts developing its own conditions</h2>
		<p>One thing I like about separating the project into Worlds is that each one gradually develops a kind of internal logic.</p>
		<p>Not necessarily strict rules.</p>
		<p>More like boundaries.</p>
		<p>Once I spend enough time inside one of them, I start recognizing when something belongs there and when I am trying too hard to force it in.</p>
		<p>199X does not need the same things as Fake/minds. Axioms should not solve its songs in the same way as Protocol 7, and when I eventually get deeper into Worlds like Moria or Soliloquium I will probably find problems I cannot even see yet.</p>
		<p>That is part of what interests me.</p>
		<p>Each record makes me learn a new language without completely forgetting the ones that came before.</p>
		<p>Because I do not want them to feel like thirteen projects made by thirteen different people either.</p>
		<p>There still has to be some trace connecting them.</p>
		<p>Sometimes it will be obvious and sometimes it probably will not.</p>

		<h2>Some songs may need to wait</h2>
		<p>This is a part I still find difficult.</p>
		<p>When I like a song, I want to find a place for it.</p>
		<p>It is very easy to start inventing reasons why it should belong to a particular record simply because I do not want to leave it out.</p>
		<p>I am trying not to do that.</p>
		<p>There are songs that will probably move around quite a lot before I understand where they belong. There will probably be others that do not end up inside any World at all, even if I like them.</p>
		<p>I think I also have to learn to accept that.</p>
		<p>The fact that a song exists does not mean the story needs it.</p>
		<p>And removing something does not necessarily mean it failed.</p>
		<p>Sometimes you are simply protecting everything around it.</p>

		<h2>Images also tell me when something feels out of place</h2>
		<p>Curiously, sometimes I understand all of this visually before I understand it musically.</p>
		<p>I begin imagining a cover or some scene connected to a song and notice that I am forcing the image too much just to make it match the World.</p>
		<p>When that happens, I usually listen again.</p>
		<p>It does not always mean the song is in the wrong place, but several times it has been a sign that I still do not really understand what I am trying to do with it.</p>
		<p>I suppose that makes sense because I am no longer separating sound and image as much as I used to.</p>
		<p>Sound helps me find images and the images end up revealing things about the sound.</p>
		<p>Sometimes I reach an answer from one side, sometimes from the other.</p>

		<div class="article-image">
			<img src={`${imageBase}/independent-artist-song-brief-production-direction.webp`} alt="A song session being viewed again after its visual direction reveals that something feels out of place." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
		</div>

		<h2>Not every song has to explain the World</h2>
		<p>I am also trying to avoid the opposite extreme.</p>
		<p>If every song has to perfectly represent the entire concept of the record, everything becomes too obvious.</p>
		<p>I do not want that.</p>
		<p>A song can be small.</p>
		<p>It can stay inside one specific moment, one emotion, or introduce something that initially does not seem very important.</p>
		<p>The weight can appear later.</p>
		<p>I think that is where the sequence becomes more interesting, because I do not have to constantly explain what everything means.</p>
		<p>I can leave information incomplete and allow another World to transform it later.</p>
		<p>Some things that appear early in Fhugawz should not be completely understood when they first appear.</p>
		<p>I am still discovering some of them myself.</p>

		<div class="article-image">
			<img src={`${imageBase}/ai-demo-reconstruction-human-music-process.webp`} alt="Listening closely to how an individual song supports the larger World around it." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
		</div>

		<h2>I think less and less about isolated songs</h2>
		<p>I think that is the main change.</p>
		<p>I still want a song to be able to work on its own. There would be no point in building all this structure just to hide songs that cannot support themselves without an explanation around them.</p>
		<p>But I also do not want to force them to exist completely alone.</p>
		<p>Now I listen while also thinking about what comes before, what remains afterward and where the character is when they reach that point.</p>
		<p>That is making some songs change a lot.</p>
		<p>Others, surprisingly, almost not at all.</p>
		<p>And whenever one finally finds its place, something happens that I like quite a lot: I stop thinking only about whether the song is finished.</p>
		<p>I start feeling that it belongs somewhere.</p>
		<p>At this point, I think that matters more to me.</p>
	</div>
</ArticleLayout>
